F — Controller / hardware recommendations around power electronics & capacitors (short)
    • GaN vs SiC:
        ◦ GaN FETs can reduce switching losses and size for very high frequency PWM and high switching rates — they’re excellent if you can afford them and care about peak efficiency & weight.
        ◦ SiC is better for very high voltages (EV scale); for 48–96V GaN is attractive for efficiency and switching speed. Recommend GaN for high-performance prototypes; provide Si MOSFETs as lower-cost fallback.
    • Caps / DC bus:
        ◦ Provide local bulk capacitance across the DC bus (before H-bridge) sized for worst-case transient energy: (E = 0.5 C (\Delta V)^2).
        ◦ Choose allowed ΔV (e.g., ±5–10% of bus) and worst-case pulse energy (from measured current pulses × voltage × pulse duration). Add calculation tool (Colab) to size C — I can provide that script next.
        ◦ Use low-ESR film caps for high-pulse performance; supplement with polymer electrolytic for bulk. Supercaps only if you intentionally want big energy buffering — expensive & heavy.
    • Protection: fast TVS, precharge resistor, inrush limit, diode/flyback handling adapted to inductive loads. Use synchronous rectification/regenerative path rather than simple flyback where practical.

G — Final recommendations / narrative
    • DE5 advantage: ~15% electrical energy saving in these examples — meaningful for range or smaller battery sizing. Absolute savings bigger at high speed (because base energy is higher).
    • People will floor it: account for user behavior: enforce safe peak durations, thermal budgets, and have clear user modes.
    • Offer both controllers: keep the 2HB/3HB switchability in firmware. Provide recommended defaults: 3HB for performance when thermal/currents allow, 2HB as fail-safe/low-power mode.
    • Logging & telemetry: essential for tuning — collect every ride to refine surrogate models and constraints.